\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Two ways a precise design can mislead. (a) A straight line fitted to data generated by the saturating model over a narrow nitrogen range (shaded): the fit is good by conventional criteria, yet extrapolating to $x = 40$ overpredicts biomass by more than a factor of two. (b) Estimates of the nitrogen effect at $n = 500$ from an analysis omitting soil moisture (naive) and one including it (adjusted); the dashed line is the true causal effect of 7. (c) The naive estimate as sample size increases from 100 to 10\,000: the interval contracts steadily around a value roughly 65\% too large.\relax }}{17}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:limits}{{8}{17}{Two ways a precise design can mislead. (a) A straight line fitted to data generated by the saturating model over a narrow nitrogen range (shaded): the fit is good by conventional criteria, yet extrapolating to $x = 40$ overpredicts biomass by more than a factor of two. (b) Estimates of the nitrogen effect at $n = 500$ from an analysis omitting soil moisture (naive) and one including it (adjusted); the dashed line is the true causal effect of 7. (c) The naive estimate as sample size increases from 100 to 10\,000: the interval contracts steadily around a value roughly 65\% too large.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
